A geometric-algebraic multigrid solver {#section:python-api-examples:multigrid}
======================================

Even though  originally had been written for convection-diffusion
equations with multigrid, most of our applications today don't use this
feature. The discussion here is thus a little bit dated and does not
really showcase what the code is capable to do.

A simple, vertex-centred Finite Differences solver with hard-coded operators
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

We start our experiments with a simple Finite Differences solver for the
Poisson equation

$$- \Delta u = f.$$

### Data model

We model our data through the  Python interface. We could alternatively
write the data model in C++ ourselves, but this is more delicate (though
it migth provide more freedom). In the simplest version of the code,
where we assume that there are no material coefficients and that we know
the right-hand side, we need two quantities per vertex: There has to be
a scalar which carries the actual solution $u$, and we need an
additional scalar to accumulate the residual. Within our Python script,
we thus create a vertex data model that only hosts these two scalars:

We add this new data model to our project:

### Plot the result

If you want to plot the result, there is a very simple plotter available
from the `toolbox`:

The plotter gives you a (series of) grid outputs. These outputs are
patch files in a block-structured AMR sense, but each block is
degenerated, i.e. is a $1\times 1$ voxel.

